    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Everyday, many children spend hours watching television and movies. When parents are present to watch and discuss media with their kids, these shows and movies can support positive learning experiences. However, children are often left to watch unsupervised while their parents attend to other tasks, reducing these learning opportunities. Discussions between parents and children around media have been shown to be vital in supporting children’s social and emotional learning that occurs with media, yet many parents find it difficult to discuss television and movies with their children when they also haven’t had the chance to watch. As a result, many children could be missing out on the benefits of watching and discussing media with their parents.\r\n\r\nThis report describes Stream, a website and streaming service plugin that provides parents with resources they need to make discussing media with their children easier. Stream provides episode summaries and question suggestions, making it possible for parents to have meaningful conversations with their children about television and film, even when they haven’t had the chance to watch themselves. Stream’s discussion guides are specifically organized around social and emotional themes, helping parents ultimately support their child’s social and emotional development.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
